WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.690
Film Courage: What key steps did you take
to go from being a tenured professor?

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:12.050
Most people would do many things that aren’t
good to be in those shoes.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.510
I’m sure first of all you had to deal with
social pressure, people were probably trying

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.199
to talk you out of it…maybe not?

00:00:18.199 --> 00:00:19.210
What steps did you take?

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.960
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Film Producer: Well…in
retrospect you can always make it look more

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.109
planned and logical than what it was at the
time.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:33.510
But I basically…I ran into a very inspiring
many whose name is Norman Cousins who was

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:40.180
the editor of Saturday Review world of those
days and he came to speak in a class of mine

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:46.120
at Occidental College and it turned out we
shared a motto that no one else in the world

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:52.239
had ever heard of and that motto was a single
sentence by the Spanish philosopher [José]

00:00:52.239 --> 00:01:01.180
Ortega y Gasset that said “I think the only
immoral thing is for a being not to use every

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:07.130
instant of its existence with the utmost intensity.”

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:13.049
And I had never heard anyone else quote that
but after his talk in my class I asked him

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:18.740
to come to my office and I showed him that
it was framed above my desk and so needless

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:24.850
to say we bonded and long story short I asked
him what I should do when I grow up which

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:32.220
I asked male authority figures all my life
basically and he told me after we got to know

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.799
each other that I should consider the entertainment
business because it was much broader than

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:44.679
the academic world and people can basically
do whatever…anything creative you’re encouraged

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:46.319
to do basically.

00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:48.780
You can find your own way.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.390
There are no rules and schedules and all of
those kind of things that we find in academia.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.609
And I love academic you know?

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:59.939
The world and the ideas that are exchanged
and all of that.

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:05.359
But it was restricting and it was (for me)
suffocating.

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:10.269
Which is a word that means a lot to me personally.

00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:16.939
It’s my most ancient nightmare being suffocated
and I’ve never been suffocated in the entertainment

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:17.939
world.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:19.840
I’ve been terrified a lot but not suffocated.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.180
And so he encouraged me and I thought well
I don’t know anything about the entertainment

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.599
world other than movies and that’s it.

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:32.930
And he showed me a passage from a book by
William Goldman that I hope everyone knows

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.400
called Adventures in the Screen Trade.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.290
And the passage was that the only important
rule in Hollywood is that nobody knows anything.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.290
And I thought “Wow?

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.299
That’s good.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:52.620
It means it’s a level playing field so I
set out to learn as much as I could.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:59.650
And I realized that I wasn’t 18-years-old
in the mailroom at William Morris and I wasn’t

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:04.230
infinitely wealthy and I didn’t have relatives
in the film business so those are like the

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.280
three ways to get into business (normally).

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.329
So I thought I just have to be smarter and
so I started reading contracts.

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:20.129
I remember a producer (I’ll never forget)
asked if I could read the distribution contract

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:24.379
and he said “Yeah, I can let you read it
but I can’t let you take it out of my office.

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:29.659
You can go out of the room and have a cappuccino…do
that.”

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:35.400
So I read and I came back an hour later and
I said “I’m confused about some things

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.400
I read here?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.819
Can I ask you a couple questions?”

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:38.819
And he said “Sure.”

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:45.980
And I said “This paragraph number 48 in
the fine print section in the end said the

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:53.790
accounting terms used in this agreement shall
be redefined by the 21th Century Fox accounting

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:59.279
department at such time if any that litigation
is entered into at such time if any that litigation

00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:00.760
is entered into among the parties.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.640
And I said “What does that mean?”

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.720
And he said “That is not in there?”

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:06.909
And I said “It is, let me show you.”

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:08.079
And I showed it to him.

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:11.650
And he said “I can’t believe that that’s
still in there?

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.139
My attorney should have crossed that out.”

00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:15.099
He had just signed the agreement?”

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:18.239
And I said “Well they didn’t.”

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:24.160
So that’s how I started learning by reading
contracts because I think whatever kind of

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.699
thing you’re trying to do if it’s successful
ends up being a bunch of contracts.

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:34.690
So you might as well start backwards with
the contracts and long story short while I

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.030
was preparing myself that way over a 6-month
period.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:45.810
I came up with an idea that I sold basically
on a wing and a prayer not knowing how to

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:53.440
do it but it ended up being over the next
12-months 16 movies that I was completely

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:01.400
in charge of and raised half the money from
Warner Brothers and half the money from a

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:07.970
company in Canada and went to Montreal and
shot them all (back to back meeting).

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:13.699
One movie ended on Friday and the next movie
ended on Friday and it was a series of romantic

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:14.699
comedies.

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:26.120
And it came out of my teaching romantic literature
and teaching publishing because a visiting

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:31.440
publisher was talking in my publishing class
and he was telling us what goes on the cover

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.419
of a romance novel and I realized as he listed
the things that were on the cover that he

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:42.960
was basically reciting the rules of courtly
love that I was teaching in another class

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:49.970
that were written in the 12th century by Andreas
Capellanus (the Chaplin of Marie de France)

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:57.470
and I thought so maybe romance novels that
everyone makes fun of are just an extension

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:02.210
of these ancient courtly stories (these love
stories).

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:09.730
So I came up with the idea of doing a series
of movies that imitated these love stories

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:18.729
and were marketing friendly because they all
had colors so you could put all of the DVDs

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:22.970
on a shelf and they would form a rainbow.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:28.069
So they were all called things like The Rose
Cafe and Sunset Court and Indigo Autumn, etc.

00:06:28.069 --> 00:06:37.280
And we did 16 of them and by that time I was
fully in the business because I was in charge

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:46.199
of production (creative production) and within
three movies my assistant and I were…we

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:50.439
knew what we were doing whereas we did not
know what we were doing before the first movie

00:06:50.439 --> 00:06:56.350
started shooting and then I came back to Los
Angeles and because a literary manager because

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:02.199
I didn’t have resources to option properties
but as a literary manager you can produce

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:10.419
properties by managing the property and that’s
what got me going ever since then.

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:17.729
So that was just because I thought of an idea
and I didn’t know better.

00:07:17.729 --> 00:07:25.610
If I’d know then what I know now I would
never have sold it the way I sold it.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:31.139
I simply went out with a concept and convinced
the several studios to look at it seriously

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:35.740
and none of them had looked at a script or
anything like that and one of them (Warner

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:41.330
Brothers) wanted to see and I wouldn’t show
it to them until they signed an agreement

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:47.500
and they ended up signing the agreement in
three days and then I manufactured the scripts

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:54.189
over the weekend by putting out a call to
the romance novel community and getting back

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:55.610
ideas for the script and so on.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:08:00.759
So it was a fluke and one of the hardest things
about being in the business when you’ve

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:10.560
been in it for awhile is there grows up this
huge accumulation of experience that you have

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.469
that makes you know that you shouldn’t just
pick up the phone and call the head of a studio

00:08:16.469 --> 00:08:18.699
and I have to overcome that.

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:22.669
I just reached out to the head of a studio
this morning.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:27.069
But every time I do that it’s like having
a 500-pound weight in your hand to pick up

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:30.370
the phone because you know that’s wrong.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:37.400
But somebody like me back then, I didn’t
know it was wrong so it was a light motion

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.620
to pick up the phone and call somebody.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:45.550
And so whenever I get a new partner who is
not involved I always say “Don’t be afraid

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:52.070
to tell me your craziest ideas” because
this is a world in which crazy ideas work

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:56.320
and it’s the traditional ideas that have
a harder time.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.340
So it is a completely wild and entrepreneurial
frontier.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.650
It’s probably the last frontier of American
culture (the movie business) and it’s been

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.360
changing ever since I’ve been in it.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.710
It constantly changes from a world in which
video cassettes dominated (you could find

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:20.720
them anywhere) to a world in which we’re
downstreaming from Netflix and Hulu and so

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.190
on.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.570
The delivery methods have always changed.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:30.260
And what doesn’t change (and this is the
encouraging thing for writers) is the need

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:36.200
for stories has only gotten greater and greater
with the proliferation of hundreds of channels.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.130
They all have one thing in common, they need
programming.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:46.250
They need content and writers are the ones
that create the content (the intellectual

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.250
property).

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.760
So they should be hugely encouraged.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.810
You don’t have to understand all of the
distribution methods you just need to know

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:56.740
how to tell a story and you’re in good shape.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:57.550
Just keep telling stories.

